#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172933} :activity/id #uuid "d69714f2-bb12-5119-931a-cf96944e88c7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049628 :topic/id #uuid "06a51304-5013-5e6f-ab88-267fa31fcd9b" :topic/math-academy-id 3372} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172933 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173175 :tutorial/content "Consider the planar curve $y=e^{-x}$ defined on the interval $x \\in [0,2],$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/a8/a8ce2449af085548fecc543fbd5ccfec0e8024344547b2a7825f3a6e9ccf63d4.png)\n\nBy rotating this curve $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, we get a **surface of revolution**:\n\n![](images/88/88c9c1ec629ab9967cc011aaadafebea255cd50abee9c3730bed4f53f671c71b.png)\n\nWe can parametrize this surface in the following way:\n\n- We take a point $P$ on the surface with $x$-coordinate equal to $x_0$ for some fixed $x_0 \\in [0,2].$\n\n- Now, by taking the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=x_0,$ we get a circle of radius $e^{-x_0}.$\n\n![](images/f1/f1e2cf1c571a21e06f1b25f99a64b5529fea8c65c897b3f225cfd0d307d725d7.png)\n\n \nThis circle can be parameterized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(x_0, \\theta) = \\big\\langle {\\color{black}x_0}, \\: e^{-x_0} \\, \\cos{\\theta}, \\: e^{-x_0} \\, \\sin{\\theta} \\big\\rangle,\n\\quad  \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\n- Finally, by allowing $x_0$ to vary over the interval $[0, 2],$ these circles will trace out the surface of revolution. Therefore, our parameterization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,\\theta) = \\big\\langle x, \\: e^{-x} \\cos{\\theta}, \\: e^{-x}\\sin{\\theta} \\big\\rangle,\n\\quad \nx \\in [0,2], \n\\quad\n\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nLet's now describe some general formulas for parametrizing surfaces of revolution." :tutorial/id #uuid "9bd13796-1bf7-5a1b-a9f4-87567fd6998c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5305 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1d490ab1-06f7-5e16-b643-b1b0d8fab4fe" :step/math-academy-id 33155 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173086}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172934 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172055 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171861 :question/id #uuid "1d32df15-74ee-5a3d-bc33-3d6452700e84" :question/math-academy-id "e-8879"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ac4af78-af9b-5d48-a5d7-7d47ad92e348" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050722}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235460} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247037} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275400}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface of Revolution: Rotation About the x-Axis"} :step/id #uuid "9771f588-30ca-519b-9cd3-5da25e6f18e1" :step/math-academy-id 33156 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172965}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172935 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172056 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171862 :question/id #uuid "6ab25451-ad5c-5437-b4ba-046b0bd30fe6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8880"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b85acdfc-0505-5472-b5f3-53270bffa030" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401083}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface of Revolution: Rotation About the y-Axis or z-Axis"} :step/id #uuid "8409e7cf-c975-5a30-a407-4fc56e97763f" :step/math-academy-id 33157 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173083}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172965 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173186 :tutorial/content "Consider the point $(x(y),y,0)$ that lies on the curve $x=x(y)\\geq 0$ for $y\\in [a,b]$ in the $xy$-plane. Rotating this point by $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis generates a circle, as shown below.\n\n![](images/98/981ed341198c54e5dffeae770d13b3d46222ad429d0315ecd057b43995b4c0d6.png)\n\nThe pair $(\\textrm{circle}_1,\\textrm{circle}_2)$ denotes any parametrization of a circle of radius $x(y)$ with parameter $y.$\n\nUsing arguments similar to those seen earlier, the corresponding surface of revolution can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x(y) \\cos\\theta, \\: y, \\: x(y) \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad y\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\n**Note:** Since a parametrization of a circle is not unique, we can obtain different parameterizations of our surface of revolution. For example, the following is also a valid parameterization:\n\\[ \n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x(y) \\sin\\theta, \\: y, \\: x(y) \\cos\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad y\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi). \n\\]\n\nSimilarly, the rotation of a point $(0,y,z(y))$ on the curve $z=z(y)\\geq 0$ in the $yz$-plane about the $y$-axis can be visualized as follows:\n\n![](images/59/5936d54e55c151ec2d9786a3ce2bd807970a71c10a21974562fd601bf50dfd37.png)\n\nThe corresponding surface of revolution can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle z(y) \\cos\\theta, \\: y, \\: z(y) \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad y\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nAs mentioned previously, neither of these parameterizations is unique.\n\n**Note:** Since a parametrization of a circle is not unique, we can obtain different parameterizations of our surface of revolution. For example, the following is also a valid parameterization:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle z(y) \\sin\\theta, \\: y, \\: z(y) \\cos\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad y\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi). \n\\]" :tutorial/id #uuid "ae882ec8-1ebd-5277-8224-b440b433c8ee" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5507 :tutorial/title "Formulas for Rotation About the y-Axis"} :step/id #uuid "23271cc4-5b18-5ab7-87a0-420c4bf3d6fc" :step/math-academy-id 34312 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173087}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173083 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172132 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171712 :question/id #uuid "8ed57b1f-0082-5281-b061-ff2020ed7d66" :question/math-academy-id "e-12364"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4df896f9-7094-5d49-a5cf-3d0f62164298" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048384}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401212}] :knowledge-point/title "The Fundamental Vector Product of a Surface of Revolution"} :step/id #uuid "2109942c-c8ff-56d5-8311-ec29a24f7390" :step/math-academy-id 44310 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173084}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173084 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173253 :tutorial/content "Recall that if $S$ is a surface parametrized by a differentiable vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle x(u,v), \\: y(u,v), \\: z(u,v) \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\ndefined over a region $D$ in the $uv$-plane, then the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\: \\textrm{d}S =  \\iint\\limits_{D} \\| \\mathbf{r}'_ u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v  \\| \\: \\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{r}'_ u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v$ is the fundamental vector product of the surface.\n\nLet's apply this formula to find the surface area generated by a surface of revolution.\n\nSuppose that the curve [math]y=f(x)[/math] for [math]x \\in [a,b][/math] is rotated [math]2\\pi[/math] radians about the [math]x[/math]-axis. The corresponding surface of revolution can be parametrized as\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x, \\: f(x) \\cos\\theta, \\: f(x) \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad (x,\\theta) \\in D, \n\\end{align*}\n\nwhere [math]D= \\big\\{ (x,\\theta) \\: : \\: x \\in[a,b], \\: \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi) \\big\\}.[/math] How can we find the area of this surface?\n\nFirst, we now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{r}'_x \n&= \\bigg\\langle \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial x}, \\: \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}, \\: \\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} \\bigg\\rangle \n= \\big\\langle 1, \\:f'(x) \\cos\\theta, \\: f'(x) \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle \\\\[10pt]\n\n\\mathbf{r}'_\\theta \n&= \\bigg\\langle \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial \\theta}, \\: \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial \\theta}, \\: \\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial \\theta} \\bigg\\rangle \n= \\big\\langle 0, \\: -f(x) \\sin\\theta, \\: f(x) \\cos\\theta \\big\\rangle\n\\end{align*}\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{r}'_x \\times \\mathbf{r}'_\\theta \n&= \n\\begin{vmatrix}\n\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\\n1 & f'(x) \\cos\\theta & f'(x) \\sin\\theta \\\\\n0 & -f(x) \\sin\\theta & f(x) \\cos\\theta\n\\end{vmatrix} \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\bigg\\langle \\begin{vmatrix} f'(x) \\cos\\theta & f'(x) \\sin\\theta \\\\ -f(x) \\sin\\theta & f(x) \\cos\\theta \\end{vmatrix}, \\: -\\begin{vmatrix} 1 & f'(x) \\sin\\theta \\\\ 0  & f(x) \\cos\\theta \\end{vmatrix}, \\: \\begin{vmatrix} 1 & f'(x) \\cos\\theta \\\\ 0 & -f(x) \\sin\\theta  \\end{vmatrix} \\bigg\\rangle \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\big\\langle f(x)f'(x)\\cos^2\\theta+f(x)f'(x)\\sin^2\\theta, \\: -f(x) \\cos\\theta, \\: -f(x)\\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\big\\langle f(x)f'(x), \\: -f(x) \\cos\\theta, \\:- f(x)\\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle\n\\end{align*}\n\nand\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_y \\times \\mathbf{r}'_\\theta \\right\\|\n&= \\sqrt{\\big( f(x)f'(x) \\big)^2 + \\big( -f(x) \\cos\\theta \\big)^2 + \\big( -f(x)\\sin\\theta \\big)^2 } \\\\[3pt]\n&= \\sqrt{\\big(f(x)\\big)^2\\big(f'(x)\\big)^2 + \\big(f(x)\\big)^2 \\big( \\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta \\big) } \\\\[3pt]\n&= \\sqrt{\\big(f(x)\\big)^2\\big(f'(x)\\big)^2 + \\big(f(x)\\big)^2 } \\\\[3pt]\n&= f(x) \\sqrt{1 + \\big(f'(x)\\big)^2 }.\n\\end{align*}\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n\\begin{align*}\nA \n&= \\iint\\limits_S \\,  \\text{d}S \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\iint\\limits_D  \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_y \\times \\mathbf{r}'_\\theta \\right\\|  \\text{d}x\\text{d}\\theta \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\int_{a}^{b} \\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f(x) \\sqrt{1 + \\big(f'(x)\\big)^2 } \\: \\text{d}\\theta \\: \\text{d}x \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\int_{a}^{b} f(x) \\sqrt{1 + \\big(f'(x)\\big)^2} \\: \\text{d}x \\cdot \\int_{0}^{2\\pi}  \\text{d}\\theta \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\int_{a}^{b} f(x) \\sqrt{1 + \\big(f'(x)\\big)^2} \\: \\text{d}x \\cdot \\big[ \\theta \\big]_{0}^{2\\pi}  \\\\[5pt]\n&= 2\\pi \\int_{a}^{b} f(x)\\sqrt{1 + \\big(f'(x)\\big)^2 } \\: \\text{d}x.\n\\end{align*}" :tutorial/id #uuid "79f42de8-4cc4-576a-b367-c08bd87f8bcd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7638 :tutorial/title "Computing a Surface Area of Revolution"} :step/id #uuid "8c37f4ed-7df7-5a6c-855e-263d81b3f5b0" :step/math-academy-id 44313 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173085}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173085 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172133 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171713 :question/id #uuid "a2ff0c76-0d26-51a2-a2fb-498ff5f85559" :question/math-academy-id "e-12365"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d7d1683-4735-5c93-9ad1-542e987aed1d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371941} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389126}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Surface of Revolution"} :step/id #uuid "3533d6b6-1b8e-5af8-b6c0-195bca89c86d" :step/math-academy-id 44314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173086 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173254 :tutorial/content "Consider the point $(x,y(x),0)$ that lies on the curve $y=y(x)$ in the $xy$-plane. Rotating this point by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis generates a circle, as shown below.\n\n![](images/86/86cbc06bd3730430665744216a702ed29cc75acae33a569e00636ee53149065d.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, the pair $(\\textrm{circle}_1,\\textrm{circle}_2)$ denotes any parametrization of a circle of radius $y(x)$ with parameter $x.$\n\nIn general, given a curve $y=y(x)\\geq 0$ for $x \\in [a,b]$ that is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, the corresponding surface of revolution can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x, \\: y(x) \\cos\\theta, \\: y(x) \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad x\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nSince a parametrization of a circle is not unique, we can obtain different parameterizations of our surface of revolution. For example, the following is also a valid parameterization:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x, \\: y(x) \\sin\\theta, \\: y(x) \\cos\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad x\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi)\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3148fb53-9ae7-5747-aa63-b79dd1bc224f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7648 :tutorial/title "Parametrizing a Surface Generated by Rotating y=y(x) About the x-Axis"} :step/id #uuid "93cfa70b-b133-5659-9bce-ab370759c49e" :step/math-academy-id 44408 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173089}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173087 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173255 :tutorial/content "Now consider the point $(x(z),0,z)$ on the curve $x=x(z)\\geq 0$ for $z\\in [a,b]$ in the $xz$-plane. Rotating this point by $2\\pi$ radians about the $z$-axis generates a circle, as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50473a5407143c7ec49ce8c192c256bbc184703010209767507e4b922e9f4c7b.png)\n\nThe corresponding surface of revolution can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x(z) \\cos\\theta, \\: x(z) \\sin\\theta, \\: z \\big\\rangle, \\quad z\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nFinally, the rotation of a point $(0,y(z),z)$ on a curve $y=y(z)\\geq 0$ in the $yz$-plane about the $z$-axis can be visualized as follows:\n\n![](images/5f/5ff1574db4d105e7d69cc12ed609898d626b4977902f98dbcbcac6633ad56d2b.png)\n\nThe corresponding surface of revolution can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle y(z) \\cos\\theta, \\: y(z) \\sin\\theta, \\: z \\big\\rangle, \\quad z\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9e8a0947-f30a-5da6-9c3b-7b93bdba56bf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7649 :tutorial/title "Formulas for Rotation About the z-Axis"} :step/id #uuid "6100252f-8102-5700-8f5a-11a2b77335a0" :step/math-academy-id 44409 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172935}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173089 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173257 :tutorial/content "Now consider the point $(x,0,z(x))$ that lies on a curve $z=z(x)$ in the $xz$-plane. Rotating this point by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis generates a circle, as shown below.\n\n![](images/74/74686e9e17ce056fea730b47bdf34ee582cc83920200f0345f75b90a6f8c9833.png)\n\nIn general, given a curve $z=z(x)\\geq 0$ for $x \\in [a,b]$ that is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, the corresponding surface of revolution can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x, \\: z(x) \\cos\\theta, \\: z(x) \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad z\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nAgain, this parametrization is not unique. An alternative parametrization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x, \\: z(x) \\sin\\theta, \\: z(x) \\cos\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad z\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c06437e5-d847-5d03-aac0-497649901444" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7714 :tutorial/title "Parametrizing a Surface Generated by Rotating z=z(x) About the x-Axis"} :step/id #uuid "3100ec93-73c0-5fba-bebc-ee40c07ece7d" :step/math-academy-id 44739 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172934}}] :activity/title "Surfaces of Revolution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172185}]}
